<p>Rent gets all the attention in UAE budgeting conversations, but three smaller categories quietly drain more money than people expect: toll gates, utilities, and food delivery apps. None of them feel expensive transaction-by-transaction — which is exactly the problem.</p>

<h2>Cutting Salik (and Darb) Costs</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Check your actual monthly total first.</strong> Most people underestimate this until they see it categorized separately from other transport costs — it's easy to treat each toll as negligible while the monthly sum tells a different story.</li>
  <li><strong>Adjust commute timing where possible.</strong> Shifting a commute by even 20–30 minutes can avoid peak charges on certain gates.</li>
  <li><strong>Reconsider routes for occasional trips.</strong> A slightly longer non-toll route is sometimes faster in practice once you factor in toll-gate congestion.</li>
  <li><strong>Carpool or use public transport for predictable, repeated routes</strong> — the Metro and bus network cover major commuter corridors and eliminate toll costs entirely for those trips.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Reducing DEWA Bills</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Air-conditioning is the single biggest lever.</strong> Raising your set temperature by even 1–2 degrees, using timers, and sealing gaps around windows/doors meaningfully reduces cooling costs.</li>
  <li><strong>Check your chiller billing separately if applicable.</strong> Some buildings bill district cooling apart from DEWA — understanding which portion is fixed vs. usage-based tells you where you actually have control.</li>
  <li><strong>Unplug idle appliances and use energy-efficient bulbs</strong> — a small effect individually, but consistent across a full apartment.</li>
  <li><strong>Compare bills month-to-month, not against a "typical" number.</strong> Your own baseline is more useful than a generic average, since usage patterns vary heavily by household.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Taming Food Delivery App Spending</h2>
<p>Delivery apps (Talabat, Deliveroo, Careem) are the category most people wildly underestimate until they see it totaled. A few orders a week, each individually reasonable, can add up to a substantial monthly figure once delivery fees, service charges, and small markups are included.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Set a hard weekly order cap</strong> rather than deciding case-by-case — decision fatigue is what drives most "just this once" orders.</li>
  <li><strong>Batch-cook one or two meals a week</strong> to cover the nights you'd otherwise default to ordering out of convenience.</li>
  <li><strong>Compare the true cost, not just the menu price</strong> — delivery fees and service charges often add a meaningful percentage on top of the listed price.</li>
  <li><strong>Use pickup/collection options</strong> when the app offers them — you skip the delivery fee entirely while still getting the convenience of ordering ahead.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The Real Fix: See the Totals, Not the Individual Charges</h2>
<p>None of these categories feel expensive one transaction at a time — that's precisely why they're easy to underestimate. The households that successfully cut these costs are the ones who see a monthly total for "Salik," "DEWA," and "Food Delivery" clearly labeled, rather than scattered among dozens of unlabeled bank line items.</p>

<blockquote>You can't fix a category you can't see clearly. Categorization, not willpower, is usually the first real lever.</blockquote>

